# MCSP Terminal Release Capability 0.45

Status: Constructive capability-condition experiment
Qualification: Under Conditional Experiment
Parent: HEX-POSSIBILITY-LAYER-0.44

## Principle

MCSP SHALL distinguish:

    current condition
    capability condition
    invoked transition

without requiring any of them to be foundational STATE.

A condition may be estimated, calculated, observed or derived without being reified as a state.

## Current condition

For this fixture the assembly program is launched from an invoking terminal/process environment and remains foreground-attached for the entire qualification run.

The fixture does not detach itself.

## Capability condition

The program may determine:

    TERMINAL_RELEASE_CAPABLE

when the applicable host realization exposes sufficient mechanisms for a future explicit terminal-release transition.

For the current Linux x86-64 realization the bounded capability calculation requires knowledge of available process/session and file-descriptor mechanisms sufficient to construct a later detachment procedure.

This is capability evidence only.

## Non-invocation rule

Capability SHALL NOT imply action.

    TERMINAL_RELEASE_CAPABLE != TERMINAL_RELEASE_INVOKED

No session detachment, fork/background continuation, terminal descriptor reassignment, daemonization or equivalent release transition is invoked by 0.45.

A future release operation requires an explicit transition request and separate qualification.

## Condition vocabulary

    TERMINAL_ATTACHED
        current invocation remains attached to its terminal/process boundary.

    TERMINAL_RELEASE_CAPABLE
        applicable realization contains a qualified route by which a later explicit operation could release terminal occupation.

    TERMINAL_RELEASE_INVOKED
        reserved for a future explicit transition; false/uninvoked in 0.45.

    BACKGROUND_OPERATING
        possible later derived condition; not established in 0.45.

These are determinations of condition, not kernel states.

## Kernel

Kernel remains DETERMINATE CONDITION.
STATE remains derived and optional.
